course: dictionary
lesson: 3059
title: Echolocation: finding things by sound
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Echolocation is a way of finding things by sound. You make a noise, then listen for the echoes that bounce back. The clue is in the word itself: echo and location, finding where something is. «Dolphins find things by echolocation: they send out fast clicks and listen to the echoes.» The clicks and the echoes tell you it is about sound.
explain: The words around a new word are its context. «Bats are not blind: all bats can see, and many also find insects in the dark by echolocation.» Blind and in the dark are the clues. A bat can find insects with no light at all, so echolocation must be a way of finding things without seeing them.
explain: A good check is to swap your own meaning into the sentence. Try «many also find insects in the dark by finding things by sound and listening for echoes». It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: Echolocation means finding things by sending out sounds and listening for the echoes that bounce back.
fact: Some animals use echolocation, such as bats and dolphins.
fact: Machines, like sonar on ships, also use echolocation to find things underwater.
fact: «Bats are not blind» means bats can see, and many also use echolocation to find insects in the dark.
fact: Context check: swap your meaning into the sentence. If «finding things by listening for echoes» still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «Dolphins find things by echolocation.» What does echolocation mean? | Finding things by smell in deep water | *Finding things by sound and listening for echoes | Swimming very fast through the sea | Hiding from other animals in the dark
exercise: «Dolphins find things by echolocation: they send out fast clicks and listen to the echoes.» Find and copy one word that shows what the dolphins send out. | things | fast | echoes | *clicks
exercise: «Many small bats find insects in the dark by echolocation.» Why can a bat find insects in the dark? | It only hunts in daylight | *Its echolocation lets it find things by sound and echoes | It has no need to listen | It carries a torch
test: «Many small insect-eating bats shout high-pitched calls and listen to the echoes, like a torch made of sound.» What does the comparison «like a torch made of sound» suggest? | Bats are made of light | Sound is too heavy to carry | *Echolocation lights up the way ahead, using sound instead of light | Bats carry torches at night
test: Which machine uses echolocation to find things underwater? | A camera | A telescope | *Sonar on a ship | A kettle
exam: Sam says echolocation means being blind. Is Sam right? | No, because dolphins cannot make sounds | Yes, echolocation means being unable to see | Yes, all bats are blind | *No, bats are not blind, and echolocation is finding things by sound and echoes
